    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "# The ancestry mosaic of the United States\n\nA replica, on this panel, of the ancestry map *The New York Times* published in July 2026. The NYT does not publish its derived dataset, but it does state its source, and that source is open: the **American Community Survey 2024 (5-year)** from the U.S. Census Bureau, a work of the federal government and therefore in the public domain. The fixtures are rebuilt with `testdata/make-ancestrydata.py` — no API key.\n\n**158 groups · 85,044 census tracts · 338 million people.** Tables\n[B04006](https://api.census.gov/data/2024/acs/acs5/groups/B04006.html) (reported ancestry),\n[B03001](https://api.census.gov/data/2024/acs/acs5/groups/B03001.html) (detailed Hispanic origin) and\n[B02015](https://api.census.gov/data/2024/acs/acs5/groups/B02015.html) (detailed Asian groups);\ngeometry from the TIGER 2024 *cartographic boundary files*.\n\n**How to read it.** Ancestry in the ACS is self-reported and multiple-response, and the three tables do not share a universe, so per-tract totals are renormalised where they overlap; the remainder shows up as *not reported*. The Census is forbidden by law from asking about religion, so groups defined by it do not exist in these data. And only seven groups get a colour of their own: a dot map is an all-against-all colour problem, and seven is the most that keeps the pairs distinguishable under colour blindness. The rest fall into *Other groups* — the bar chart and the table show what is inside, and the choropleth reaches all 158 one at a time.\n\n*Pan and zoom on the main map: the other panels follow its viewport.*"
      },
      "transparent": true
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "type": "ubica-keplergl-panel",
      "title": "The mosaic · one dot = 250 people",
      "description": "Dot density, the technique that reproduces the effect of the NYT map: kepler does not blend colours, but the overlap of neighbouring dots reads as a blend. This panel sends its viewport to the rest of the dashboard through viewportVariables.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
        "uid": "duckdb"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 16,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 8
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "motherduck-duckdb-datasource",
            "uid": "duckdb"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "format": 1,
          "rawQuery": true,
          "rawSql": "-- Un punto por cada 250 habitantes, coloreado por su grupo.\n-- Dos recortes, no uno. El primero es el encuadre del mapa: kepler escribe\n-- west/south/east/north y DuckDB poda por estadísticas de row-group, porque el\n-- Parquet está ordenado espacialmente (celda H3 res 3).\n-- El segundo es de densidad. A escala metropolitana entran los cuatro buckets y\n-- Chicago rinde ~28.000 puntos; a escala nacional serían 1,35 millones y 86 MB,\n-- así que se queda un bucket de cada cuatro. Los buckets recorren la secuencia\n-- de puntos de cada tract, que está ordenada por grupo, de modo que cualquier\n-- subconjunto conserva las proporciones (verificado: ±0,5 puntos).\nSELECT latitude, longitude, ancestry, color_group, state\nFROM read_parquet('/testdata/ancestry/dots.parquet')\nWHERE longitude BETWEEN ${west:raw} AND ${east:raw}\n  AND latitude  BETWEEN ${south:raw} AND ${north:raw}\n  AND bucket < CASE\n        WHEN (${east:raw} - (${west:raw})) * (${north:raw} - (${south:raw})) > 100 THEN 1\n        WHEN (${east:raw} - (${west:raw})) * (${north:raw} - (${south:raw})) > 5   THEN 2\n        ELSE 4 END",
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
